WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.504
- What's up Believe Nation it's Evan.

00:00:01.504 --> 00:00:03.215
My one word is believe and I believe

00:00:03.215 --> 00:00:06.079
that you have the ability to
do something really special,

00:00:06.079 --> 00:00:09.137
and I want to play, a
small part in your success

00:00:09.137 --> 00:00:10.864
with these videos that I'm making.

00:00:10.864 --> 00:00:12.992
And so to help you on your
journey, today I'm continuing the

00:00:12.992 --> 00:00:15.120
How Billionaires Think,

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:19.224
Success Advice From the
Top Series, Volume Three.

00:00:19.224 --> 00:00:21.381
And as always guys as you're
watching if you hear something

00:00:21.381 --> 00:00:22.587
that really resonates with you,

00:00:22.587 --> 00:00:24.361
please leave it down
in the comments below.

00:00:24.361 --> 00:00:26.697
Put quotes around it so
other people can be inspired.

00:00:26.697 --> 00:00:27.737
And when you write it down,

00:00:27.737 --> 00:00:30.652
it's much more likely to
stick with yourself as well.

00:00:30.652 --> 00:00:32.253
Enjoy!

00:00:32.253 --> 00:00:34.920
(dramatic boom)

00:00:36.891 --> 00:00:40.997
(intense, inspiring melody)

00:00:40.997 --> 00:00:42.913
- The thing about being
an entrepreneur is,

00:00:42.913 --> 00:00:44.463
it's just, all to you.

00:00:44.463 --> 00:00:46.565
You know a lot of people
like to make excuses,

00:00:46.565 --> 00:00:47.784
I don't have connections,
I don't have money,

00:00:47.784 --> 00:00:49.504
I don't have this, but, you know,

00:00:49.504 --> 00:00:52.741
if you find something that
you like to do or love to do,

00:00:52.741 --> 00:00:54.224
be great at it,

00:00:54.224 --> 00:00:56.355
and see if you can turn
it into a business.

00:00:56.355 --> 00:00:57.457
And worst case,

00:00:57.457 --> 00:00:59.095
you're going to have fun doing
what it is you love to do.

00:00:59.095 --> 00:01:00.786
And best case, you can
turn it into a business.

00:01:00.786 --> 00:01:03.312
I'm just not big on excuses,
I just think if you're really,

00:01:03.312 --> 00:01:05.140
everybody has that
opportunity to go for it

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:07.223
they just got to to do it.

00:01:08.793 --> 00:01:12.605
(intense, inspiring melody)

00:01:12.605 --> 00:01:16.017
- Turn your wounds into wisdom.

00:01:16.017 --> 00:01:18.859
You will be wounded
many times in your life.

00:01:18.859 --> 00:01:20.212
You'll make mistakes.

00:01:20.212 --> 00:01:22.298
Some people will call them failures.

00:01:22.298 --> 00:01:24.209
But I have learned that failure,

00:01:24.209 --> 00:01:26.137
is really God's way of saying,

00:01:26.137 --> 00:01:29.334
"Excuse me, you're moving
in the wrong direction."

00:01:29.334 --> 00:01:33.501
It's just an experience.
(crowd applauds loudly)

00:01:35.549 --> 00:01:37.132
Just an experience.

00:01:38.417 --> 00:01:40.202
Just an experience.

00:01:40.202 --> 00:01:42.952
I remember being taken
off the air in Baltimore,

00:01:42.952 --> 00:01:46.999
being told that, I was no
longer being fit for television,

00:01:46.999 --> 00:01:48.859
and that I could not anchor the news

00:01:48.859 --> 00:01:50.653
because I used to go out on the stories.

00:01:50.653 --> 00:01:52.283
And my own truth was,

00:01:52.283 --> 00:01:54.008
even though I'm not a weeper,

00:01:54.008 --> 00:01:56.471
I would cry for the people in the stories.

00:01:56.471 --> 00:02:00.058
Which really wasn't very
effective as a news reporter

00:02:00.058 --> 00:02:02.896
to be covering a fire.
(audience laughs)

00:02:02.896 --> 00:02:07.403
And crying because the
people lost their house.

00:02:07.403 --> 00:02:09.060
And it wasn't until,

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:12.227
I was demoted as a on-air anchorwoman,

00:02:13.364 --> 00:02:17.219
and thrown into the talk
show arena to get rid of me,

00:02:17.219 --> 00:02:20.259
that I allowed my own
truth to come through.

00:02:20.259 --> 00:02:21.480
And the first day I was on the air,

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:24.631
doing my first talk show back in 1978,

00:02:24.631 --> 00:02:26.447
it felt like breathing.

00:02:26.447 --> 00:02:29.473
Which is what your true
passion should feel like.

00:02:29.473 --> 00:02:32.277
It should be so natural to you.

00:02:32.277 --> 00:02:34.703
And so I took what had been a mistake,

00:02:34.703 --> 00:02:37.350
what had been perceived as a failure

00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:40.286
with my career as a anchorwoman
in the news business

00:02:40.286 --> 00:02:42.564
and turned it into a talk show career

00:02:42.564 --> 00:02:44.564
that's done okay for me.

00:02:45.986 --> 00:02:49.653
(intense, inspiring melody)

00:02:51.028 --> 00:02:53.258
- You are unusually fearless and willing

00:02:53.258 --> 00:02:54.529
to go in the face of other people

00:02:54.529 --> 00:02:55.664
telling you something is crazy.

00:02:55.664 --> 00:02:56.810
And I know a lot of,

00:02:56.810 --> 00:02:58.427
pretty crazy people, you still stand out.

00:02:58.427 --> 00:03:00.450
Where does that come from
or how do you think about,

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:02.654
making a decision when everyone tells you

00:03:02.654 --> 00:03:03.964
this is a crazy idea?

00:03:03.964 --> 00:03:06.445
Or where do you get the
internal strength to do that?

00:03:06.445 --> 00:03:08.936
- Well first of all I'd say I actually

00:03:08.936 --> 00:03:11.603
think I feel fear quite strongly.

00:03:11.603 --> 00:03:15.446
So it's not as though I just
have the absence of fear

00:03:15.446 --> 00:03:17.529
I feel it quite strongly.

00:03:18.457 --> 00:03:21.536
But there are just times when
something is important enough,

00:03:21.536 --> 00:03:23.022
you believe in it enough,

00:03:23.022 --> 00:03:25.914
that you do it in spite of fear.

00:03:25.914 --> 00:03:28.018
- So speaking of important things--

00:03:28.018 --> 00:03:30.562
- Like people shouldn't think I,

00:03:30.562 --> 00:03:33.175
People shouldn't think well
I feel fear about this,

00:03:33.175 --> 00:03:34.839
and therefore I shouldn't do it.

00:03:34.839 --> 00:03:37.343
It's normal to feel fear.

00:03:37.343 --> 00:03:38.698
Like you'd have to,

00:03:38.698 --> 00:03:40.444
definitely something
mentally wrong with you

00:03:40.444 --> 00:03:42.204
(laughs) that you shouldn't feel fear.

00:03:42.204 --> 00:03:44.866
- So you just feel it and
let the importance of it,

00:03:44.866 --> 00:03:46.190
drive you to do it anyway?

00:03:46.190 --> 00:03:48.621
- Yeah, you know actually something

00:03:48.621 --> 00:03:50.294
that can be helpful is Fatalism.

00:03:50.294 --> 00:03:51.389
At some degree.

00:03:51.389 --> 00:03:53.433
If you just accept the probabilities,

00:03:53.433 --> 00:03:55.683
then, that diminishes fear.

00:03:57.085 --> 00:03:59.085
So, when starting SpaceX

00:04:00.548 --> 00:04:04.835
I thought the odds of
success were less than 10%,

00:04:04.835 --> 00:04:07.391
and I just accepted
that actually, probably

00:04:07.391 --> 00:04:09.808
I would just lose everything.

00:04:11.214 --> 00:04:13.399
But that maybe we'd make some progress,

00:04:13.399 --> 00:04:15.242
if we could just move the ball forward.

00:04:15.242 --> 00:04:16.493
Even if we died, maybe,

00:04:16.493 --> 00:04:19.601
some other company could
pick up the baton and move,

00:04:19.601 --> 00:04:21.250
and keep moving it forward.

00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:23.500
So we'd still do some good.

00:04:25.088 --> 00:04:27.212
Yeah, same with Tesla, I thought the odds

00:04:27.212 --> 00:04:31.578
of a car company succeeding
were extremely low.

00:04:31.578 --> 00:04:35.245
(intense, inspiring melody)

00:04:36.254 --> 00:04:38.970
- So many things go wrong,
when you're starting a company,

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:40.526
and often I think people ask,

00:04:40.526 --> 00:04:43.602
"What mistakes should you avoid making?"

00:04:43.602 --> 00:04:45.565
And my answer to that question is

00:04:45.565 --> 00:04:47.590
don't even bother trying
to avoid mistakes,

00:04:47.590 --> 00:04:50.892
because you're going to
to make tons of mistakes.

00:04:50.892 --> 00:04:52.260
The important thing is
actually learning quickly

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:55.803
from whatever mistakes you
make and not giving up.

00:04:55.803 --> 00:04:58.501
And, I mean there are
things every single year

00:04:58.501 --> 00:05:00.716
of Facebook's existence
that could've killed us,

00:05:00.716 --> 00:05:01.752
or made it so that,

00:05:01.752 --> 00:05:03.204
it just seemed like moving forward

00:05:03.204 --> 00:05:05.509
or making a lot of progress
just seemed intractable.

00:05:05.509 --> 00:05:07.755
But you just kind of
bounce back and you learn.

00:05:07.755 --> 00:05:10.189
Nothing is impossible
you just have to kind of

00:05:10.189 --> 00:05:13.581
keep running through the walls.

00:05:13.581 --> 00:05:17.248
(intense, inspiring melody)

00:05:19.237 --> 00:05:20.904
- Does ideas matter?

00:05:21.803 --> 00:05:23.436
Now people say of course ideas matter.

00:05:23.436 --> 00:05:28.335
What kind of stupid life
lesson is it that ideas matter?

00:05:28.335 --> 00:05:29.764
The truth of the matter is

00:05:29.764 --> 00:05:32.536
this is both under and over-appreciated.

00:05:32.536 --> 00:05:35.488
Most people think that
every idea is a good idea,

00:05:35.488 --> 00:05:37.273
as long as they had it.

00:05:37.273 --> 00:05:38.957
(audience laughs)

00:05:38.957 --> 00:05:40.207
The truth of the matter is,

00:05:40.207 --> 00:05:41.978
not every idea is a good idea,

00:05:41.978 --> 00:05:43.820
not every idea matters,

00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:46.699
and not every idea should be pursued.

00:05:46.699 --> 00:05:48.444
And if you're in a field like I am,

00:05:48.444 --> 00:05:50.644
or was, of innovation,

00:05:50.644 --> 00:05:53.066
not every idea is a good idea.

00:05:53.066 --> 00:05:55.939
Even if it strikes you
that way on first blush.

00:05:55.939 --> 00:05:58.304
In fact most people are
lucky to have one idea

00:05:58.304 --> 00:06:00.897
that really matters in their whole life.

00:06:00.897 --> 00:06:02.905
That's not a criticism.

00:06:02.905 --> 00:06:05.234
But earth-shattering, life-changing ideas

00:06:05.234 --> 00:06:07.300
are very few and far between.

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:11.290
Research insights, investment
insights, innovations,

00:06:11.290 --> 00:06:15.955
a good idea needs to be
cherished and nurtured.

00:06:15.955 --> 00:06:19.622
(intense, inspiring melody)

00:06:21.426 --> 00:06:24.184
- Most important thing is you have to--

00:06:24.184 --> 00:06:25.662
there's two sides,
there's really sort of the

00:06:25.662 --> 00:06:27.305
intellectual or mental side.

00:06:27.305 --> 00:06:30.619
You have to pick a
business that has a niche.

00:06:30.619 --> 00:06:32.407
You have to have a reason to succeed.

00:06:32.407 --> 00:06:34.096
You have to be something
like we were doing, say

00:06:34.096 --> 00:06:36.342
Japan can beat Germany in manufacturing

00:06:36.342 --> 00:06:37.575
at this type of a product.

00:06:37.575 --> 00:06:38.814
That was the niche really.

00:06:38.814 --> 00:06:40.128
And then, on that,

00:06:40.128 --> 00:06:42.327
you just have to have a
total and complete passion.

00:06:42.327 --> 00:06:45.976
Because there's going to
be so many dark moments.

00:06:45.976 --> 00:06:47.660
You know, I think that the dark moments

00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:51.569
are true for any entrepreneur,
or any start-up company.

00:06:51.569 --> 00:06:54.122
I'm sure that if you'd
talk to Steve Jobs at Apple

00:06:54.122 --> 00:06:56.836
you'd see many, many, dark, dark moments.

00:06:56.836 --> 00:06:58.548
Ours happened to last a little
longer than most of them,

00:06:58.548 --> 00:07:01.586
but you really emotionally
have to be prepared

00:07:01.586 --> 00:07:02.849
for those dark moments.

00:07:02.849 --> 00:07:06.178
- I think, every entrepreneur
I've ever spoken to

00:07:06.178 --> 00:07:08.471
would echo that.
- Yeah.

00:07:08.471 --> 00:07:09.890
- That there are dark moments when

00:07:09.890 --> 00:07:11.100
everything's on the line.

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:12.854
- That's the life of an entrepreneur.

00:07:12.854 --> 00:07:13.954
And if you're going to be into it

00:07:13.954 --> 00:07:16.962
you better be prepared for that.

00:07:16.962 --> 00:07:21.425
(intense, inspiring melody)

00:07:21.425 --> 00:07:24.202
- Going against the tide
is great if it works.

00:07:24.202 --> 00:07:25.684
Now it doesn't always work.

00:07:25.684 --> 00:07:28.710
And you really have to be
smart to go against the tide,

00:07:28.710 --> 00:07:30.957
because you're going against
a lot of smart people.

00:07:30.957 --> 00:07:34.474
But when you get it right,
you'll end up better.

00:07:34.474 --> 00:07:36.480
Whether it's Microsoft or Google,

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:37.660
I mean so many people,

00:07:37.660 --> 00:07:40.781
that have really hit big
have gone against the tide.

00:07:40.781 --> 00:07:43.037
But you have to have great
confidence in yourself,

00:07:43.037 --> 00:07:45.164
you have to be the right person.

00:07:45.164 --> 00:07:47.200
There are very few people
that can pull it off,

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:49.652
but when you pull it off, it's great.

00:07:49.652 --> 00:07:50.799
Same thing in real estate.

00:07:50.799 --> 00:07:52.180
You go against the tide.

00:07:52.180 --> 00:07:56.925
People are selling, you're
buying, the tide changes.

00:07:56.925 --> 00:08:00.592
(intense, inspiring melody)

00:08:01.745 --> 00:08:04.861
- The fundamental
connection to doing business

00:08:04.861 --> 00:08:07.289
is basically relationships.

00:08:07.289 --> 00:08:09.606
You know, people really, if
you really think about it,

00:08:09.606 --> 00:08:12.955
people want to do business
with people they like.

00:08:12.955 --> 00:08:16.259
I mean every now and then
you'll do a deal with the devil,

00:08:16.259 --> 00:08:17.592
but you don't really like the devil,

00:08:17.592 --> 00:08:19.354
but you do it sometimes.
(laughing)

00:08:19.354 --> 00:08:20.634
But you really much rather do business

00:08:20.634 --> 00:08:21.816
with somebody you like.

00:08:21.816 --> 00:08:23.138
Somebody who shares your values,

00:08:23.138 --> 00:08:24.561
somebody who you know

00:08:24.561 --> 00:08:27.356
is committed to the
same objectives you are.

00:08:27.356 --> 00:08:29.860
Somebody who's willing to
work as hard as you are.

00:08:29.860 --> 00:08:31.672
And when you find those people,

00:08:31.672 --> 00:08:34.309
those are the people you
want to bring under your tent

00:08:34.309 --> 00:08:36.854
to be part of your business objective.

00:08:36.854 --> 00:08:39.979
And, you've got to do that by,

00:08:39.979 --> 00:08:42.203
you know sort of presenting
yourself in a way,

00:08:42.203 --> 00:08:44.170
that people clearly understand

00:08:44.170 --> 00:08:47.672
that you are committed to creating value.

00:08:47.672 --> 00:08:50.147
Business people want to create value.

00:08:50.147 --> 00:08:51.585
Doesn't mean that they
don't want to have fun,

00:08:51.585 --> 00:08:54.004
doesn't mean that they don't want to be

00:08:54.004 --> 00:08:56.393
involved in other kinds of things.

00:08:56.393 --> 00:09:00.194
But if you can go to
someone and convince them,

00:09:00.194 --> 00:09:02.903
that I can help you create value,

00:09:02.903 --> 00:09:06.518
and by your ability to articulate that

00:09:06.518 --> 00:09:07.943
and then to communicate it,

00:09:07.943 --> 00:09:09.510
and to give people the impression

00:09:09.510 --> 00:09:13.254
that you will work as hard as they will,

00:09:13.254 --> 00:09:14.737
to create that value,

00:09:14.737 --> 00:09:17.462
you're going to find people
wanting to come to you.

00:09:17.462 --> 00:09:19.921
And so, for me it's sort of been like that

00:09:19.921 --> 00:09:21.790
throughout my whole career.

00:09:21.790 --> 00:09:24.373
Is being able to say to people,

00:09:25.587 --> 00:09:28.920
talk to people in a way
that convinces them,

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:31.684
that I am going to do everything I can,

00:09:31.684 --> 00:09:34.931
to help make what they
want to achieve a success.

00:09:34.931 --> 00:09:37.254
And by extension I'm
going to be a success.

00:09:37.254 --> 00:09:38.837
When I started BET,

00:09:40.942 --> 00:09:43.736
the guy who invested in BET
is a guy named John Malone,

00:09:43.736 --> 00:09:45.298
he was at that time the operator,

00:09:45.298 --> 00:09:49.023
the third largest cable
company in Denver Colorado.

00:09:49.023 --> 00:09:51.442
And he kept investing money into BET

00:09:51.442 --> 00:09:53.279
without taking additional equity.

00:09:53.279 --> 00:09:55.943
And as BET became a big success,

00:09:55.943 --> 00:09:59.412
I ended up with about 75% of the company,

00:09:59.412 --> 00:10:02.110
he ended up with about 25% of the company.

00:10:02.110 --> 00:10:03.805
And one day I asked him
I said, "John, John,

00:10:03.805 --> 00:10:07.100
"you put over a half a
million dollars in BET

00:10:07.100 --> 00:10:09.134
"as a startup, you put it in as debt,

00:10:09.134 --> 00:10:10.885
"you didn't put it in as equity."

00:10:10.885 --> 00:10:12.873
Which would've given
him greater ownership.

00:10:12.873 --> 00:10:15.362
I said, "Why'd you do it that way?"

00:10:15.362 --> 00:10:17.480
And he said, "Bob I always knew,

00:10:17.480 --> 00:10:18.802
"you would work harder for yourself

00:10:18.802 --> 00:10:21.040
"than you would for me."

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:22.692
And it was that kind of commitment.

00:10:22.692 --> 00:10:24.814
So if you could convey to people,

00:10:24.814 --> 00:10:28.327
that you're willing to work hard for them,

00:10:28.327 --> 00:10:29.895
and for yourself,

00:10:29.895 --> 00:10:31.238
you got a business partner.

00:10:31.238 --> 00:10:33.488
You're connecting the dots.

00:10:34.615 --> 00:10:38.282
(intense, inspiring melody)

00:10:39.413 --> 00:10:41.397
- You know there's a
very thin dividing line

00:10:41.397 --> 00:10:42.997
between success and failure,

00:10:42.997 --> 00:10:46.858
when you start a business from scratch.

00:10:46.858 --> 00:10:48.725
If you don't have enough
money to pay the bills,

00:10:48.725 --> 00:10:51.199
you've gone to the wrong
side of that dividing line.

00:10:51.199 --> 00:10:53.518
And if you can somehow get
enough money to pay the bills,

00:10:53.518 --> 00:10:57.351
you stay the right side
of that dividing line.

00:11:00.229 --> 00:11:04.396
I've always been running
faster than perhaps I should,

00:11:05.642 --> 00:11:08.796
and I've had great difficultly
saying no to things

00:11:08.796 --> 00:11:12.381
so I keep on taking on new things.

00:11:12.381 --> 00:11:14.954
And we've just managed
to stay the right side

00:11:14.954 --> 00:11:18.436
of the dividing line over the
last 50 years in business.

00:11:18.436 --> 00:11:21.213
But, equally in those early days

00:11:21.213 --> 00:11:23.544
when you're starting a business

00:11:23.544 --> 00:11:25.449
without any financial backing,

00:11:25.449 --> 00:11:28.784
it's easy to slip the wrong side.

00:11:28.784 --> 00:11:32.928
And in fact, most entrepreneurs
do slip the wrong side.

00:11:32.928 --> 00:11:37.095
The important thing is then
just to pick yourself up

00:11:37.929 --> 00:11:39.335
and start again.

00:11:39.335 --> 00:11:41.104
And if it doesn't work at that time,

00:11:41.104 --> 00:11:45.271
keep going until you do
succeed and not to give up.

00:11:46.930 --> 00:11:50.597
(intense, inspiring melody)

00:11:51.669 --> 00:11:54.661
- I want to talk about
dreams for a second.

00:11:54.661 --> 00:11:55.842
And in my case,

00:11:55.842 --> 00:11:58.031
literally a dream.

00:11:58.031 --> 00:11:59.842
When I was in college,

00:11:59.842 --> 00:12:04.009
I was sure that I'd been
admitted by a clerical error.

00:12:04.912 --> 00:12:07.937
Probably a computer error.
(crowd laughing)

00:12:07.937 --> 00:12:10.785
And because of that I
had an irrational fear,

00:12:10.785 --> 00:12:13.118
I'd be sent home on the bus.

00:12:14.055 --> 00:12:18.095
And (mumbles) knows this is true.

00:12:18.095 --> 00:12:19.199
But it turns out,

00:12:19.199 --> 00:12:20.928
because of that anxiety,

00:12:20.928 --> 00:12:24.196
I woke up literally with a dream.

00:12:24.196 --> 00:12:26.634
And it was kind of a strange dream.

00:12:26.634 --> 00:12:28.242
It went like,

00:12:28.242 --> 00:12:31.279
I think I could download the entire web,

00:12:31.279 --> 00:12:34.708
onto some multiple computers
that were lying around.

00:12:34.708 --> 00:12:39.413
And, that could probably seem
pretty crazy to most people.

00:12:39.413 --> 00:12:42.150
But I stayed up a couple of
hours in the middle of the night

00:12:42.150 --> 00:12:46.819
doing some math, and it seemed
actually pretty plausible.

00:12:46.819 --> 00:12:48.860
While assuming you actually didn't keep

00:12:48.860 --> 00:12:50.787
any of the web pages.

00:12:50.787 --> 00:12:52.743
You only kept the links.

00:12:52.743 --> 00:12:56.050
And then, sort of figured out,

00:12:56.050 --> 00:12:59.538
given all that data I thought
it would take a couple weeks.

00:12:59.538 --> 00:13:02.298
And, I told my advisor that.

00:13:02.298 --> 00:13:05.379
And he just sort of laughed at me.

00:13:05.379 --> 00:13:07.915
And of course it took a year or two.

00:13:07.915 --> 00:13:09.583
But at the end of that,

00:13:09.583 --> 00:13:12.419
we had a way to rank web pages,

00:13:12.419 --> 00:13:15.158
and no thoughts to search at all.

00:13:15.158 --> 00:13:18.478
But eventually, search
entered the picture.

00:13:18.478 --> 00:13:19.948
And you know the rest.

00:13:19.948 --> 00:13:21.778
And that became Google.

00:13:21.778 --> 00:13:23.655
(audience clapping)

00:13:23.655 --> 00:13:25.420
So I'd like to encourage everyone,

00:13:25.420 --> 00:13:26.995
to follow their dreams.

00:13:26.995 --> 00:13:28.539
- Thank you guys so much for watching!

00:13:28.539 --> 00:13:29.573
I hope you enjoyed.

00:13:29.573 --> 00:13:31.118
I'd love to know what did you think,

00:13:31.118 --> 00:13:32.545
what did you take from this video,

00:13:32.545 --> 00:13:35.374
what was the most memorable
or most applicable lesson

00:13:35.374 --> 00:13:37.768
that you're going to immediately implement

00:13:37.768 --> 00:13:39.914
into your life or your business somehow.

00:13:39.914 --> 00:13:41.284
Let me know down in the comments below.

00:13:41.284 --> 00:13:43.787
I'm looking forward to hearing
what you guys have to say.

00:13:43.787 --> 00:13:45.288
Thank you guys again for watching.

00:13:45.288 --> 00:13:46.373
I believe in you.

00:13:46.373 --> 00:13:48.325
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:13:48.325 --> 00:13:50.339
and whatever your one word is.

00:13:50.339 --> 00:13:53.117
Much love, see you soon.

00:13:53.117 --> 00:13:55.427
(dramatic boom)

